{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>For you went up onto your father's bed.</b> He explains here that what is written, ״וישכב את בלהה״ — the intent is not actual lying down, but rather that he disturbed his father's bedding, as in the Midrash of our Rabbis, of blessed memory. And this is why he seized him only on account of the 'going up': 'then you defiled' — he who went up onto the bedding. And the meaning of the verse is: when you went up onto your father's bedding, then you defiled your father who is supreme, who was accustomed to ascend upon the bedding — this is the Shekhinah, whose way is to ascend upon the bedding; and therefore he mentioned 'my beddings' (משכבי) in the plural. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in the chapter 'במה בהמה יוצאה' — others say: two beddings he disturbed, one of his father and one of the Shekhinah, as it is said: 'for you went up onto your father's beddings.' And the others needed to say thus because the Shekhinah is constantly present with him and never departs from his thought forever. As our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted (Genesis 30:16): ״וישכב עמה בלילה הוא״ — it does not say 'that one' but 'he' (הוא), which teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, assisted him in that act. From here you learn that everyone who honors his father honors HaShem, and everyone who disgraces the honor of his father — behold, this one profanes the Name. And this is why he mentioned here 'you defiled' (חללת), for the honor of his father and mother and their reverence — Scripture weighed them against the honor of the Place (המקום) and His reverence. As our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: three partners have a share in a person — the Holy One, blessed be He, and his father and his mother. And when a person honors his father and mother, the Holy One, blessed be He, says: I account it to you as though I dwelt among you and you honored Me." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Then you defiled my bedding — went up.' Jacob said to him: My son, you have no remedy until there comes the one about whom it is written (Exodus 19:3): ״ומשה עלה אל האלהים״. And once Moses came and stood on the mountain, he brought up and counted the tribe of Reuben first among the curses, and the tribe of Reuben opened its mouth and said: ״ארור שוכב עם אשת אביו״ — 'Cursed be he who lies with his father's wife.' All Israel knew that Reuben was righteous until here." "<b>And you must understand</b> that Reuben alludes to the attribute of Judgment below, and I have already mentioned this in the account of the birth of the tribes, and there I wrote what is alluded to in the name of each and every one. And this is the sense of כחי וראשית אוני. And likewise פחז כמים — an expression of haste, like the rush of waters."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:29.340033327Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}